{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "clara-web",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-27",
  "compatibility_flags": [
    // Enables Node.js APIs in the Worker runtime — required by @opennextjs/cloudflare.
    // https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/configuration/compatibility-flags/#nodejs-compatibility-flag
    "nodejs_compat",
    // https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/configuration/compatibility-flags/#global-fetch-strictly-public
    "global_fetch_strictly_public"
  ],
  // THE WORKER'S NON-SECRET CONFIGURATION, WITH A HOME IN THE REPO.
  //
  // Until this block existed, every one of these lived only in the Cloudflare account:
  // nothing in the repo said what the deployed Worker was configured with, and nothing
  // went red when a value was missing or wrong. That is the exact shape the 2026-07-26
  // intake incident punished (docs/ops/incident-2026-07-26-intake-storage.md:41-56) — a
  // single unset runtime URL, invisible in the tree, diagnosed only from a failing upload.
  //
  // WHAT MAY LIVE HERE: values that are PUBLIC FACTS about this deployment. A secret must
  // never appear in this file — it is committed. Secrets stay with `wrangler secret put`
  // and are listed (names only) in apps/web/.env.example: SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY,
  // RESEND_API_KEY, CLARA_RATE_WALL_PEPPER, CLARA_AUTH_WALL_SERVICE_TOKEN,
  // STRIPE_SECRET_KEY.
  //
  // ONE NAME, ONE HOME. A name declared here is NOT also set with `wrangler secret put`;
  // the deploy-time list in the FS-10 cutover record drops these three accordingly. And
  // the reason is stronger than tidiness: THIS BLOCK REPLACES the Worker's plain-text
  // variables on every `wrangler deploy` / versions upload. Whatever someone typed into
  // the Cloudflare dashboard for one of these names is gone at the next upload, silently
  // and with no diff to read — so the dashboard is not a second home for them, it is a
  // value that will be overwritten. Change the name's value HERE, in a PR, or not at all.
  //
  // FS-10 POSTURE FOR `CLARA_PUBLIC_ORIGINS`, ruled by the lead: it ships NARROW — the
  // real app origin only — from the FIRST upload, including the preview. A preview served
  // on a workers.dev hostname therefore REFUSES every same-origin-walled POST, and that
  // refusal is not a misconfiguration to work around: it is the wall's positive proof,
  // and the ceremony records it as such. `readSameOriginConfig` does accept a
  // comma-separated list (lib/same-origin.ts:72-75 — it reads the name at :73 and splits
  // on "," at :75), but the ruling is NOT to reach for it for a preview — widening
  // the allowlist to make a preview convenient is how the wall stops being a wall, and
  // the widened value is the one that would then ride to production in this file.
  //
  // INVITE_MAIL_FROM is deliberately ABSENT: it is not a secret, but its value is a
  // deployment fact (the verified Resend sending address) that this repo does not state,
  // and inventing one here would be worse than leaving it to the ceremony. Its name's
  // repo-visible home is .env.example.
  "vars": {
    // The runtime origin the same-origin proxy forwards to, read at REQUEST time by
    // app/api/runtime/[...path]/route.ts. Since the FS-10 pre-cutover fix this carries
    // the chat and task-stream legs as well as intake, the bytes viewer and the
    // interview runner — i.e. every runtime call the browser makes. Unset ⇒ a typed
    // 503 and no chat at all, which is why it belongs somewhere a reader can see it.
    "CLARA_RUNTIME_URL": "https://clara-runtime.fly.dev",
    // The exact public origins this app answers as (scheme + host, comma-separated).
    // lib/same-origin.ts compares the browser's Origin against this list rather than
    // trusting X-Forwarded-Host, which is written by whoever spoke to us.
    "CLARA_PUBLIC_ORIGINS": "https://app.clarabook.com",
    // The Stripe mode this deployment declares, read at REQUEST time by
    // lib/checkout/stripe-session.ts, which refuses to create a Checkout Session
    // when the class of STRIPE_SECRET_KEY (sk_live_/rk_live_ vs sk_test_/rk_test_)
    // disagrees with it — in either direction. Unset ⇒ checkout refuses, because
    // "the deployment has not stated its mode" must never mean "accept anything"
    // on a money surface.
    //
    // NOT A SECRET (it is a public fact about this deployment), which is why it
    // lives here and not with `wrangler secret put` — the key itself stays a
    // secret. THE SAME NAME AND THE SAME CLOSED VOCABULARY the runtime's webhook
    // route already gates on (packages/runtime/lib/stripe-livemode.mjs), so both
    // arms of one deployment read one fact; the value is spelled the way the
    // runtime's own deploy env spells it.
    //
    // `test` FOR THE WHOLE BETA (裁-126/148): Stripe stays on the BELCORT sandbox
    // account until the real-money switch, which is a deliberate, owner-run act —
    // flipping this to `live` is one step of that ceremony (with KYB and the
    // non-zero checkout walk), never a drive-by edit.
    "CLARA_STRIPE_LIVEMODE": "test",
    // On apps/web this is CF-Connecting-IP — the header THIS app's own Cloudflare edge
    // sets. Never X-Forwarded-For: any client can send it, and a wall keyed on a
    // client-settable header is a form field the attacker fills in. The RUNTIME's
    // same-named variable takes a different correct value (X-Clara-Client-IP); see
    // apps/web/.env.example for why the two differ.
    "CLARA_TRUSTED_CLIENT_IP_HEADER": "CF-Connecting-IP"
  },
  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  }
  // No KV/R2/D1/service bindings yet — P1 is the foundation scaffold only.
  // Add bindings here (and mirror them in open-next.config.ts) when a real
  // deploy needs them.
}
